      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Purpose:",
          "content": "To analyze the use of this sponge in pediatric patients undergoing split-liver transplantation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Methods:",
          "content": "Retrospective study, including 35 pediatric patients undergoing split-liver transplantation, divided into two groups according to the use of the sponge: 18 patients in Group A (no sponge) and 17 in Group B (with sponge)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Results:",
          "content": "The characteristics of recipients and donors were similar. We observed greater number of reoperation due to bleeding in the wound area in Group A (10 patients - 55.5%) than in Group B (3 patients - 17.6%); p = 0.035. The median volume of red blood cells transfused in Group A was significantly higher (73.4 ± 102.38 mL/kg) than that in Group B (35.1 ± 41.67 mL/kg); p = 0.048. Regarding bile leak there was no statistical difference."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Conclusion:",
          "content": "The use of the human fibrinogen and thrombin sponge, required lower volume of red blood cell transfusion and presented lower reoperation rates due to bleeding in the wound area."
        }
      ]},
